Falsifies `device_tree_blob::verification::be32_is_total`.

The defect: the checked add the function's own comment names goes back to a bare one. `be32`
is a leaf reader over an untrusted blob, so `at` is attacker-controlled; near `usize::MAX` the
bare `at + 4` overflows and panics before `bytes.get` ever runs, which is exactly the crash the
`checked_add` was written to close. Milestone 18 (verify the capability core, then spread inward)
is where the hardening landed.

Expected to fail: `attempt to add with overflow`, at the `let end = at + 4;` line, for any `at`
Kani picks near `usize::MAX`. The harness's own body is nothing but the call, so there is
nothing else in it that could fail first.

diff --git a/crates/device_tree_blob/src/lib.rs b/crates/device_tree_blob/src/lib.rs
index 6b3324864..39bcd1d8b 100644
--- a/crates/device_tree_blob/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/device_tree_blob/src/lib.rs
@@ -112,7 +112,7 @@ fn be32(bytes: &[u8], at: usize) -> Result<u32, Error> {
     // `at + 4` is a checked add, not a bare one: `at` comes straight out of the (untrusted) blob,
     // and a near-`usize::MAX` offset would otherwise panic on the overflow before `get` ever runs.
     // Proved total in the verification module.
-    let end = at.checked_add(4).ok_or(Error::Truncated)?;
+    let end = at + 4;
     let slice = bytes.get(at..end).ok_or(Error::Truncated)?;
     Ok(u32::from_be_bytes(slice.try_into().unwrap()))
 }
